nce":92,"evidenceIds":["E12","E2"]},{"itemId":"I23","answer":"sufficient","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E8","E14","E15"]}],"evidenceRequests":["Q3","Q2","Q1"],"summary":"The OTP decline is real (E12 confirms AVL) and is explained by schedule and road changes, not absenteeism. Group C, with no recovery cut and no construction, stayed flat at about 82%. Recovery-cut routes fell about 10 points, and construction routes fell about 31 points, with step-downs timed to 6 April and 20 April. The 40% absence rise is largely a ShiftLine recording artifact: partial-day entries make up 238 of the 245 monthly increase and full-day absences rose only 1.1%. The independent dispatch log shows no change in unstaffed trips or late pull-outs. Ridership and dwell times are flat. Preferred actions: restate absences, restore recovery time, and set construction running times. The proposed attendance discipline is harmful. Suspending OTP reporting is also harmful."}}